lesson physics-the-sun-that-does-not-set v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=1 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The sun that does not set"
  goal "Use the lean of the Earth's axis to work out where the sun stays up all day and how high it stands."

  look {
    scene 36x16 {
      row all at=canvas(0, 0) space=1 align=end {
        tilt e places=[80, 45, 0] season=0 circles=1 names=1
        hut h pictures=0
      }
    }
    say """
      The Earth turns once a day about an axis that leans 23.5° from upright. On 21 June the north
      end leans towards the sun. A place goes round its dashed circle as the Earth turns: the hut at
      80° N stays on the lit side all the way round, so the sun never sets, while the equator is half
      in day and half in night. The sun is overhead at 23.5° N, and 1° lower for each degree away.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.tilt-sun count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    level easy {
      worked physics.tilt-noon lat=70 s=0 level=medium
    }
    practice physics.tilt-noon count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.midnight-first count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.tilt-midnight count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.arctic-circle count=1 seed=1 hard-seed=2
    practice physics.no-tilt count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2
  }
  story {
    show physics.hut-sails-south lat=60 easy-lat=70 hard-lat=50
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.summer-nearest
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.hut-latitude lat=80 hard-lat=75
  }
  remember {
    say "The Earth's axis leans, so in June the far north stays in the sunlight as the Earth turns."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Two ideas get in the way here. Many adults and children believe summer comes from the Earth
    being nearer the sun, and the two-star question takes that on with the real distances: the
    Earth is nearest in January. The other is that the sun "goes round" the sky; the dashed circles
    show the place going round instead. Listen for the child tracing a circle with a finger and
    asking whether any of it crosses into the night. If the degrees stall the child, stay with the
    first question of each kind and draw the lean with a pencil through an orange. Off the page,
    shine a torch on a ball held with a leaning pencil through it, turn it, and watch a spot near
    the top stay lit. The three-star question is solved by adding the two heights, which gives
    twice the lean, and then working back to the latitude.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level everything is on 21 June, a worked example finds a noon height first, the far north is at 75 to 80 degrees, the last-drawn midnight sun is simpler, and the harbour on the voyage is still inside the Arctic circle."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the questions are on 21 December as well, when the far north has no sun at all, the what-if leans are not the Earth's, the harbour is far to the south, and the three-star hut is at 75 degrees."
  }
}
